// u12 Torts. Eleven lessons: intentional torts; their defenses and privileges; negligence (duty and standard of care; landowners,
// distress and economic loss; causation; multiple tortfeasors and vicarious liability; the plaintiff's fault and damages); strict
// liability and nuisance; products liability; defamation and privacy; misrepresentation and interference. NCBE says about half of
// the Torts questions are on negligence (lessons 3-7). Source: research/torts.md (all fact patterns are our own).
//
// UNDER REVIEW (status "unsure": never drawn by the Question of the Day or the mocks). A reviewer must check:
//  - u12-h-battery-held (INT-3): contact with a cup held by the plaintiff as battery (Rest. 2d §18 cmt. c); standard black letter, not re-read.
//  - u12-r-loss-of-chance: the split between the traditional 50% threshold and proportional recovery; states divide, no majority count verified.
//  - u12-h-crime-landlord (CAU-7): a third party's crime as superseding cause; landlord duty and foreseeability vary by state.
//  - u12-h-doorman (VIC-3): employer liability for an employee's excessive force where force is part of the job (Rest. 2d Agency §228(1)(d)).
//  - u12-h-foul-ball (DEF-4): the limited-duty rule for foul balls when screened seats are offered; states differ.
//  - u12-h-sensitive-use (STR-6): strict liability for abnormally dangerous activities and an unusually sensitive plaintiff (Rest. 2d §524A); one case summary.
//  - u12-h-reasonable-alternative (PRO-4): Rest. 3d §2(b) reasonable-alternative-design test; the stem states the test, but many states keep consumer expectations.
//  - u12-h-limited-purpose (DFM-5): limited-purpose public figure and a statement unrelated to the controversy; some courts ask only for germaneness.
//  - u12-h-negligent-misrepresentation (MIS-2): Rest. 2d §552 limited-group rule versus Ultramares and foreseeability states.
//  - u12-r-other-privileges: parental discipline, citizen's arrest and recapture of chattels are standard black letter, but only secondary sources were read, and the edge cases (mistake about whether a felony occurred; goods held by a defaulting buyer) vary by state.
//  - u12-h-recapture: forcible recapture of chattels (fresh pursuit; a buyer who defaults has possession by consent); secondary sources only, not re-read.
//  - u12-h-trespasser-strict: Rest. 2d §520B (no strict liability to trespassers on the land); a treatise confirms §520A is ground damage from aircraft, but §520B's text was not found; re-read §§520A-520C.
//  - u12-r-animals: Rest. 2d §504 as quoted in Williams v. Goodwin (Cal. Ct. App. 1974) covers harm to the possessor and household; the highway and fence-out limits and §511 (no strict liability to trespassers for wild animals) are from memory; re-read §§504-511.
//
// Damages, survival, mitigation and consortium items carry no `cat`: the NCBE outline does not file them under category II, so they must not count toward negligence's share in the mixes.
// NextGen differences (exam: ["mbe"]): animals, last clear chance, survival and wrongful death, interference with business relations,
// employer-and-agent vicarious liability (moved to Business Associations II) and innocent misrepresentation.
// Contributory negligence is named in u12-r-fault-stance only as the stem-stated exception; NextGen does not list it.
// Items checked against primary text (status "book"): the Supreme Court defamation, privacy and East River opinions read in research/torts.md.
